{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 3\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "896c0bdfc105e9dd8bc5f6351de21f72a5df9984c4aab1771ff6a28bd9b0f855"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5\\sin(3t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334634" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\cos(3t)-\\sin(3t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(4\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 4\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "896c0bdfc105e9dd8bc5f6351de21f72a5df9984c4aab1771ff6a28bd9b0f855"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\cos(4t)+2\\sin(4t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334635" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\cos(4t)-2\\sin(4t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3t$ | $1-3t$ |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "896c0bdfc105e9dd8bc5f6351de21f72a5df9984c4aab1771ff6a28bd9b0f855"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-334729" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-335093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-335093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2t} & (1-2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/state.json" "file_sha256" "52a707e304f9deadca99ef8e036ad57c02932c7a555a687132dc121fefe02f2f"} {"question" "q-335093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066533/activity-metadata.json"